WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.880
Narrator: From a car left
sitting for six years

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.860
to a flooded leather bag

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.680
and a boat cover left
outside to face the elements,

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.860
these forgotten items became
breeding grounds for mold.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.840
We spoke with experts to find out

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.165
how they cleaned and sanitized them.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.990
Our first expert is Brent
Dicesare from WD Detailing.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.953
He shows us how he and his team

00:00:26.953 --> 00:00:30.090
clean a car filled with
rainwater and mold.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:32.775
They start by pumping oxygen into the car

00:00:32.775 --> 00:00:34.620
using an ozone machine.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:38.040
The oxygen suffocates and kills bacteria.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.945
They remove the seats and vacuum out

00:00:39.945 --> 00:00:42.237
all of the water that
has pooled on the floor.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.590
Because the sunroof of
the car won't close,

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.863
rainwater has dripped inside
and collected for six years.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.570
Brent uses an air compressor

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:03.510
with a Tornador attachment
to blast the carpet fibers

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.940
with an all-purpose
cleaner mixed with water.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.310
Then he sprays extractor
soap on the carpet

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:11.850
before using an extractor
to shoot it with hot water.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.520
The extra moisture will
help to reduce friction

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.190
as he uses a drill brush
to agitate the soap.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.893
Too much friction could
further damage the carpet.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.677
After agitating, he uses the extractor

00:01:24.677 --> 00:01:27.290
to suck the soap and
water out of the carpet.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:31.860
The team sprays the doors

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.860
with a bacteria-killing
solution called Tri-Clean

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:38.121
before scrubbing them down
with a Tornador attachment.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.410
To dry the doors, they
use a microfiber cloth

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.473
and another Tornador
attachment that blows out air.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.460
They vacuum the seats to
collect any surface debris

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.610
and then spray them
with a leather cleaner.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.020
Then they use a Colourlock brush

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.210
to scrub deep into the pores of the seats,

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.460
making sure they don't
use too much pressure.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.950
The team sprays the seats
again and uses a steamer

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.873
to kill any lingering bacteria.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:12.870
To clean the wheels,

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.730
they start by pressure washing them

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.470
to remove initial dirt and grime.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.810
Then they spray them with heavy acid,

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.363
agitate them, and wash away the solution.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:26.100
Mold has even accumulated
on the exterior of the car,

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.840
so they pressure wash it twice,

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.630
applying paint-safe
degreaser between sessions.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:34.410
Finally, they spray soap onto
the car using a foam cannon,

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.690
wipe it down with microfiber mitts,

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.550
and rinse it with water.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.583
To prevent a car stored
outside from becoming moldy,

00:02:41.583 --> 00:02:44.511
it's best to store it
under a car cover or tarp.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.030
Our next expert is Charlotte Staerck

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.590
from The Handbag Clinic.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.617
She demonstrates how her team
restores a leather handbag

00:02:55.617 --> 00:02:58.685
that looks unsalvageable
due to mold growth.

00:02:58.685 --> 00:02:59.850
This handbag was sitting

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:02.550
in the back of its owner's
wardrobe when a pipe burst,

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:06.060
leaving the perfect
environment for mold to spread.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:07.710
After assessing the handbag,

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:09.540
an artisan from Charlotte's team begins

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:13.200
scraping the mold away using
a variety of hand tools.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.975
Metal tools can be used in areas

00:03:14.975 --> 00:03:16.770
where the leather is harder-wearing

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:19.440
or the mold is not sticking to the bag.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.730
Otherwise, a wooden tool is used

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:23.498
to avoid scratching the leather.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.810
Next, they use a brush to remove

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.498
any dry mold left over after scraping.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.040
The artisan sprays a mold-removal solution

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.740
onto the bag to ensure the mold is killed

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.111
so it won't grow back.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:45.003
Finally, they apply an
in-house cleaner to the bag

00:03:45.003 --> 00:03:47.790
to remove any harsh
chemicals or remaining dirt.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:50.657
And then it's ready to
be returned to its owner.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:00.240
This cover certainly did its job

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.800
protecting a boat from the elements,

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.260
but now it needs a good cleaning.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:05.940
Mike Sadowski from Chet's Cleaning

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:07.590
gets it looking as good as new.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.300
Mike sprays the moldy boat cover

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.340
with a chemical solution called DOT

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.550
and then allows it to dwell
for at least 30 minutes.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:18.090
Then he uses a machine
called a Cimex to agitate.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.880
The Cimex gently pulls apart
the fibers of the boat cover

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:24.003
so that dirt, mold, and
grime can be released.

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:28.680
He power washes with soap and water

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.510
to further break up the dirt,

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:32.010
and then he uses a broom

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:34.440
to push the pooled water
off of the boat cover.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.083
This helps him see whether
he's missed any spots.

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:41.430
Mike puts the boat cover into a bath

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:42.840
to rinse off the chemicals

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:44.760
and then runs it through a rinse wringer

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.940
to squeeze out the water and
power wash it one more time

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:49.203
before hanging it to dry.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:53.700
Mike recommends getting
boat covers cleaned

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.650
when they start to look dirty.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:58.320
Applying water repellent
can also prolong the life

00:04:58.320 --> 00:04:59.653
of a boat cover.

